    <div class="jr-answer">
      <h2>The short answer</h2>
      <p><strong>Collagen has no known interaction with thyroid medication, and no published trial has ever tested the two together, so the practical issue is timing rather than safety.</strong> Take your thyroid tablet on an empty stomach, wait 30 to 60 minutes, then have your collagen shot; if the product also contains calcium or iron, leave four hours.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Nothing has been tested directly:</strong> there are no randomised trials of collagen peptides with levothyroxine. Every four-hour rule circulating online is borrowed from the calcium and iron evidence, not measured on collagen.</li>
        <li><strong>The window you already have:</strong> the American Thyroid Association's 2014 guideline puts levothyroxine 30 to 60 minutes before breakfast, or at bedtime at least three hours after dinner. A collagen shot fits outside that window without any extra rule.</li>
        <li><strong>Calcium is the ingredient with real evidence:</strong> in 20 patients who took 1,200mg of elemental calcium as calcium carbonate alongside levothyroxine for three months, T4 absorption fell and TSH rose (<em>JAMA</em>, 2000).</li>
        <li><strong>The honest limitation:</strong> Collabloom contains no calcium, iron or iodine, but it does contain 45mcg of biotin. That is about 111 times below the 5mg daily dose shown to shift thyroid test results, though nobody has tested 45mcg specifically.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

