      <h2 id="interaction">Does collagen interact with thyroid medication?</h2>
      <p>There is no documented interaction between collagen peptides and levothyroxine, and there is also no trial. Those two statements need to sit next to each other, because most pages answering this question present the first as though it were the second.</p>
      <p>We searched for a controlled study of hydrolysed collagen taken with levothyroxine and found none. The 2021 systematic review of levothyroxine interactions with food and dietary supplements in <em>Pharmaceuticals</em> catalogues what has been measured — fibre, soy, coffee, calcium, iron, grapefruit, papaya — and collagen is not among them. So the correct answer is not "collagen is proven safe with levothyroxine"; it is "collagen has never been tested, and there is no mechanism or case report pointing at a problem".</p>
      <p>That matters because the question people are really asking is bigger than the one they type. Hypothyroidism is common in India, and it is common in exactly the group that buys collagen. An eight-city study of 5,360 Indian adults found hypothyroidism in 10.95%, with 3.47% previously undetected, and anti-TPO antibodies in 21.85%. A community study of 282 postmenopausal women in South Delhi found 27.3% hypothyroid, a third of them undiagnosed until the study tested them. A great many people reaching for a collagen shot for hair fall or dull skin are either on levothyroxine already or should be.</p>

