      <h2 id="timing">How long should you wait after your thyroid tablet?</h2>
      <p>Thirty to sixty minutes is enough for a plain collagen product, and you almost certainly already keep that gap. The American Thyroid Association's 2014 hypothyroidism guideline asks for levothyroxine to be taken consistently 30 to 60 minutes before breakfast, or at bedtime three or more hours after the evening meal. A collagen shot is food in this context: 8g of protein and 35 kcal in a 30ml serving of Collabloom. Have it with or after breakfast and the question answers itself.</p>
      <p>The reason protein and food matter at all is absorption, not chemistry. Levothyroxine is absorbed in the upper small intestine and is sensitive to what is in the gut with it. Coffee is the clearest illustration: in a 2008 study in <em>Thyroid</em>, espresso swallowed at the same moment as the tablet interfered with absorption, and the effect was not present when the espresso came 60 minutes later. The same study found the effect varied between people.</p>
      <p>The four-hour figure that appears on almost every supplement blog is not a collagen finding. It is standard pharmacy guidance for calcium and iron, which bind levothyroxine directly. It is good advice for a product containing those minerals, and it is simply copied across when the product does not. If you want the general principle for a collagen shot rather than the thyroid-specific one, we have written separately on <a href="/pages/best-time-to-take-collagen">the best time to take collagen</a>.</p>

