      <p>Two things in that table are worth saying out loud. The first is that the only ingredient with a controlled trial against levothyroxine behind it is calcium — and it is in our own joint product, not the skin one. If you take levothyroxine and Skelecoll, its 250mg of calcium is a genuine reason to keep four hours between the tablet and the shot. We would rather print that than let you find it out from a pharmacist. We have written about how little that dose of supplemental calcium does for bone outcomes anyway in <a href="/pages/do-calcium-supplements-work">our piece on whether calcium supplements work</a>.</p>
      <p>The second is the vitamin C line, which cuts the other way and is still not a reason to change anything. In a 2014 study in the <em>Journal of Clinical Endocrinology and Metabolism</em>, 31 patients with hypothyroidism and gastritis took vitamin C with their levothyroxine, and the authors concluded that it enhanced absorption and should be considered where absorption is a problem. The dose is reported inconsistently in secondary sources, so we are not quoting one. The American Thyroid Association does not recommend taking vitamin C with levothyroxine as routine practice, and Collabloom's 250mg of amla-derived vitamin C is formulated to support the body's own collagen synthesis, not to be taken with a tablet.</p>

