      <h2 id="blood-test">Will a collagen shot affect your thyroid blood test?</h2>
      <p>Collagen peptides will not. Biotin can, and that is the one real laboratory risk hiding in the hair, skin and nails aisle.</p>
      <p>Biotin is used as a tag in many immunoassays, so taking it in large amounts can push a result up or down depending on how the assay is built. A 2020 study in <em>Medicine</em> gave 5mg or 10mg a day for seven days and found positive or negative interference across thyroid tests on Roche and Beckman systems, though not on one Abbott platform. A separate study in <em>Thyroid</em> found interference with TSH, free T4, total T3 and thyroglobulin at a 10mg daily dose, and no further interference 24 hours after the last dose. The manufacturer of one widely used platform advises against drawing samples from patients taking more than 5mg a day within eight hours of the last dose. The US Food and Drug Administration has issued a safety communication about biotin interference across lab tests generally.</p>
      <p>Put that next to the label. Collabloom contains 45mcg of biotin, which is 0.045mg — roughly 111 times below the 5mg dose at which interference has been demonstrated. The honest caveat is that no one has tested 45mcg against a thyroid assay, so this is dose arithmetic rather than a study. The practical risk lies elsewhere: dedicated biotin hair tablets are commonly sold at 5,000mcg to 10,000mcg, which is 5mg to 10mg — precisely the range that moves results. If you take one of those, tell the lab. We cover the same trap from the hair side in <a href="/pages/blood-tests-for-hair-fall">which blood tests to get before buying a hair supplement</a>.</p>

      <h2 id="iodine">Does marine collagen contain iodine?</h2>
      <p>Hydrolysed marine collagen is not an iodine supplement, because it is not made from seaweed. Commercial marine collagen is extracted from fish skin, scales and bones, and the hydrolysis step breaks the protein into peptides; iodine is not part of that material in any meaningful amount.</p>
      <p>We have to be careful about how firmly we say that. Searching for a measurement, what comes back is manufacturers stating that their collagen contains no added iodide or that their testing found none detectable — statements made by sellers, not independent published assays. We could not find a peer-reviewed measurement of iodine in marine collagen peptides, and we are not going to cite a figure we did not see.</p>
      <p>What you can check yourself is the label, and it is the check that actually protects you. Plenty of "marine" beauty supplements include sea kelp extract, which <em>is</em> an iodine source and is sometimes added precisely for that reason. Collabloom contains no kelp, no seaweed and no added iodide; its marine input is 8,000mg of Japanese hydrolysed marine collagen from fish. That also makes it unsuitable if you avoid fish — the detail we cover in <a href="/pages/is-collagen-vegetarian">is collagen vegetarian</a>. If you have thyroid disease and have been told to watch your iodine intake, take the label to your endocrinologist rather than taking a brand's word, ours included.</p>

      <h2 id="doctor">When hair fall is a thyroid problem, not a collagen problem</h2>
      <p>This is the part of the question that matters more than the timing, and it is the part no supplement page writes. The symptoms that send people looking for collagen are also the symptoms of an untreated thyroid: diffuse hair shedding, dry skin, and thin, brittle nails are all recognised features of an underactive thyroid, and they generally improve when the thyroid itself is treated. Shedding can also lag months behind the thyroid change, which is why the timeline rarely points at the cause.</p>
      <p>A collagen shot will not correct a low thyroid hormone level, and taking one instead of getting a TSH checked costs you months. In practice the sequence is: get the bloods, treat what the bloods show, and then decide whether a supplement is adding anything on top. Our article on <a href="/pages/collagen-for-hair">collagen for hair</a> sets out what collagen can and cannot do for shedding once the medical causes are excluded.</p>
      <p><strong>See a doctor rather than buying a supplement if</strong> your hair is shedding diffusely across the whole scalp or your parting is visibly widening; you are unusually tired, cold, constipated, or have gained weight without a change in diet; your periods have become heavy or irregular; your nails have become brittle or your skin unusually dry over months; you have a swelling in the front of your neck or any change in your voice or swallowing; or you are already on levothyroxine and your symptoms have returned. If you are on thyroid medication and starting any supplement, tell your prescriber — not because collagen is dangerous, but because your dose is titrated against blood tests, and anything that changes absorption or distorts an assay is their business.</p>

