        { "@type": "Question", "name": "Can I take collagen with levothyroxine?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Yes, with a gap. No published trial has tested collagen peptides against levothyroxine, and collagen has no documented chemical interaction with it. The caution is the same as for any food or protein: take the tablet on an empty stomach, leave 30 to 60 minutes, then have the shot." } },
        { "@type": "Question", "name": "How long after my thyroid tablet can I have a collagen shot?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Thirty to sixty minutes is enough for a plain collagen product. That is the window the American Thyroid Association's 2014 guideline already asks you to keep clear before breakfast, so a collagen shot taken with or after breakfast needs no extra planning. Leave four hours if the product also contains calcium or iron." } },
        { "@type": "Question", "name": "Does collagen affect thyroid blood test results?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Collagen peptides themselves do not. Biotin can, and many hair and nail supplements carry it in milligram doses. Collabloom contains 45mcg of biotin, which is roughly 111 times below the 5mg daily dose that shifted thyroid results in a 2020 study in Medicine, but a separate high-dose biotin tablet is a real risk to a test." } },
        { "@type": "Question", "name": "Should I stop my collagen before a thyroid test?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "There is no need to stop a plain collagen product. If you take a separate biotin supplement of 5mg a day or more, the manufacturer of one widely used assay platform advises against drawing samples within eight hours of the last dose, and one study found interference had cleared 24 hours after the last dose. Tell the lab what you take and follow your doctor's instruction." } },
        { "@type": "Question", "name": "Does marine collagen contain iodine?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Hydrolysed marine collagen is made from fish skin and scales, not from seaweed, so it is not an iodine supplement. No independent published measurement of iodine in marine collagen peptides could be found, so the honest check is the label: a product listing kelp, seaweed or added iodide is a different matter. Collabloom contains none of those." } },
        { "@type": "Question", "name": "Will collagen help the hair fall caused by an underactive thyroid?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Not on its own, and it is not a substitute for treatment. Diffuse shedding is a recognised sign of an underactive thyroid, along with dry skin and brittle nails, and these generally settle once the thyroid itself is treated. If your hair fall started before you had a TSH checked, get the test first." } },
        { "@type": "Question", "name": "Is collagen safe if I have Hashimoto's thyroiditis?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "No trial has tested collagen peptides in thyroid autoimmunity, in either direction. Collagen peptides are a protein, not an immune-modifying drug, and nothing published suggests they alter thyroid antibodies. Anti-TPO antibodies were found in 21.85% of adults in an eight-city Indian study, so this is a common question, and it is one to put to your endocrinologist." } }
